# update-expected.py's directory walk (plan 5.17.5, whose update-expected.py
# entry the section 12 row of 2026-10-30 extends): G7 runs update-expected.py
# --check --levels O0,O2 on the directories that 'gate-select.py
# expected-dirs' prints, msil/test/harness and its subdirectories among them
# when harness self-tests change.
# (a) Nothing at or below msil/test/harness of this source tree is built or
#     checked: the self-tests there are built with stand-in tools
#     (prog/composite.test links with -Wl,--stand-in-native), so they have no
#     native oracle. Nested and repeated harness directories (%S, %S/prog and
#     %S/legs/.., which is %S) give one line, 'skip (harness self-test:
#     stand-in tools): <%S>', also with the inner directory first, and the
#     skip counts as a match (exit status 0). The compiler is
#     Inputs/levels-cc.py without an --at, which fails whenever it runs
#     ('levels-cc: error: no --at for -O0'), so a skip that stops working
#     fails the test at once and never writes an expected file into the
#     source tree. Each run's stdout is its log; its stderr, where
#     update-expected.py also warns when LIB is not set, goes to a file that
#     must not hold the stand-in's error.
# (b) A file named below that directory (prog/composite.test) gets the same
#     line, also when writing, and nothing is written: composite.expected is
#     unchanged and no per-OS expected file appears beside it.
# (c) The self-tests' copies are processed: they lie below the build tree's
#     .../msil/test/harness/Output/, so the skip compares source-tree paths,
#     not the name 'harness'. Each test is processed once however the
#     arguments nest. Of '%t.w/sub %t.w %t.w/sub/prog.c', the walk of %t.w
#     covers %t.w/sub, and prog.c is processed as a named file (it has no RUN
#     line, so a walk would skip it) where it is named, after top.c; a
#     --check run that names prog.c first reports its changed expected file
#     first and once (one MISMATCH), then top.c. A directory below another
#     argument's Inputs/, which that argument's walk does not enter, is
#     processed on its own. The compiler and program stand-in is the .NET
#     Framework executable of update-expected-native-ldflags.test
#     (Inputs/fake-native-44.il with fake-native-cc.il; the program exits
#     with 44), so (c) runs where that test runs: system-windows with
#     msil-triple-windows and msil-netfx.

# DEFINE: %{walk_failing_cc} = %python %S/Inputs/levels-cc.py

# (a)
# RUN: %{harness_update_expected} --clang '%{walk_failing_cc}' --rtlib none --check --levels O0,O2 %S %S/prog %S/legs/.. > %t.a1.log 2> %t.a1.err
# RUN: %{harness_check} contains %t.a1.log '\Askip \(harness self-test: stand-in tools\): .+[/\\]msil[/\\]test[/\\]harness\n\Z'
# RUN: %{harness_check} lacks %t.a1.err "levels-cc: error"
# RUN: %{harness_update_expected} --clang '%{walk_failing_cc}' --rtlib none --check --levels O0,O2 %S/prog %S > %t.a2.log 2> %t.a2.err
# RUN: %{harness_check} contains %t.a2.log '\Askip \(harness self-test: stand-in tools\): .+[/\\]msil[/\\]test[/\\]harness\n\Z'
# RUN: %{harness_check} lacks %t.a2.err "levels-cc: error"

# (b)
# RUN: cat %S/prog/composite.expected > %t.b.before
# RUN: %{harness_update_expected} --clang '%{walk_failing_cc}' --rtlib none %S/prog/composite.test > %t.b.log 2> %t.b.err
# RUN: %{harness_check} contains %t.b.log '\Askip \(harness self-test: stand-in tools\): .+[/\\]msil[/\\]test[/\\]harness[/\\]prog[/\\]composite\.test\n\Z'
# RUN: %{harness_check} lacks %t.b.err "levels-cc: error"
# RUN: diff %t.b.before %S/prog/composite.expected
# RUN: %{harness_check} missing %S/prog/composite.windows.expected
# RUN: %{harness_check} missing %S/prog/composite.linux.expected

# (c)
